      <div class="seg s1" style="flex:1"><div class="segv" id="cgIn">$0</div><div class="segl">you put in</div></div>
      <div class="seg s2" style="flex:1"><div class="segv" id="cgGrow">$0</div><div class="segl">growth on top</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="hmca-note"><strong>The returns are an estimate, not a promise.</strong> Real markets bounce up and down and some years lose money. This tool assumes a steady average to show the shape of compounding. It is not a guarantee, and it is not personal advice.</p>
</div>

<h2>Why the growth bar eventually dwarfs your deposits</h2>
<p>In the early years, almost all of your balance is money you put in yourself. That feels slow, and it is where most people quit. But keep going and something flips. The growth starts earning its own growth, and after a couple of decades the "growth on top" bar can tower over everything you actually deposited. That crossover is the whole game. Your only job is to start early, keep feeding it, and refuse to touch it.</p>

<h2>Small numbers, long time, big result</h2>
<p>Two hundred dollars a month does not feel life-changing. But at a 7% average over 30 years, those steady deposits can grow into a genuinely serious number, and most of it is growth you never worked a single hour for. That is why we say time is the real superpower here, not the size of your paycheck. Change the years in the box above from 10 to 20 to 30 and watch how the ending number does not just double, it explodes.</p>

<p><em>This is general education, not personal advice, so check with a licensed financial professional about your situation.</em></p>

